{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/375874-what-s-irem-like-with-your-game-alternate-irems","id":375874,"title":"What's Irem like with your game? / Alternate Irems?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-01-19T21:31:05-08:00","id":375874,"title":"What's Irem like with your game? / Alternate Irems?","content":"I thought it would be cool to have Irem actually be prehistoric . . . or from the moon haha. But more seriously, how far do you generally have Irem's influence reach? There are mummies found all over the world, so having it just be found in what's now Egypt/Northern Africa seems like a bit of a waste. If your Irem never gained too much power, were there other societies like Irem elsewhere, or where those mummies meant for . . .different purposes? How resistant to other cultures do you keep Irem? Is it all Irem all the time or did it respond to other cultures by enveloping their art and belief in it's own?<br /> <br /> I haven't read much of the Storyteller's part of the book, but I thought it would be an interesting discussion. As well as any downright AU version of Irem, like, you know, Gentry occupation of human lands before being Banished and becoming Charlatans who used Irem to fuel their own immortality, or y'know, whatever else. . ..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2230-murder-chan","name":"Murder-chan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2230_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-19T21:46:58-08:00","id":375889,"title":"","content":"I like my Irem purely Egyptian/North African through and through. The idea that I should assign mystical significance to other variants of preserved body around the world, some of which owe far more to the conditions they died/were disposed of in for their current state than any form of ritualised burial, is something I tend to resist quite strongly. If you look at it, there's really nothing in common between a Mesoamerican and an Egyptian Mummy beyond the fact that English uses the word &quot;Mummy&quot; to describe each. But I had little rant on this subject a week or two ago, so I'll focus on the other side of the post.<br /> <br /> As I suspect will be the case in most Mummy games, Irem appears in mine only through instances of momentary flashbacks. I aim to keep the precise geography of any location involved vague and dream-like, the characters see only the places that are important and seem to glide between those spaces without any of the intervening space. The buildings seem impossibly large, particularly the pillars that dominate the skyline. It's a place of monoliths built at the orders of alien demigods birthed by the stars themselves, constantly on the verge of being swallowed by the sands. If I can, I try to give a not-quite-real vibe to the experience, and the sense that the memory may be distorted from the truth of Irem by the sheer importance the Arisen assign to it, if nothing else.<br /> <br /> In my personal notes, it's a bit more dry (pun totally intended). It was an empire reminiscent of Ancient Egypt in many respects. I keep to the geography outlined in the core book (roughly speaking, Turkey-Afghanistan-Sudan-Libya as its respective extremes) and expect my player characters, and Mummy NPCs, to all hail from somewhere within the empire's borders. I've not got a whole lot of information on everyday life, preferring to keep that to the realm of subjective player perspectives, but slaves, grand guild halls, a sense of life being cheap, sand, and the presence of the restless dead are common themes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-20T00:49:32-08:00","id":375954,"title":"","content":"I kinda like the idea of having Irem as a fairly small proto-Egyptian kingdom. That way, despite what the books say, Irem can be a real part of the historical record, I think it gives the setting a bit more organic cohesiveness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-20T00:52:28-08:00","id":375955,"title":"","content":"It's gone, gone, gone. Facts about Irem are literally unknowable in the context of an actual game and its player characters. The game, the way I run it, is more focused on the things the player characters did during those long, cold millenia of <i>actual </i>history, not Irem. The Ionian revolution. The siege of Vienna. Early Christianity. The Liangzhu culture. That sort of thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-20T02:44:30-08:00","id":375996,"title":"","content":"Take the idealized myth of Rome at its height and slap an Egyptian coat of paint on the whole thing. I have it spanning the entirety of North Africa and venturing far enough east to have had contact with proto-Scythian horse nomads in what's now Iran, with conflicts with various city-states in the Fertile Crescent. I haven't decided on whether or not Iremite explorers reached the Americans, but I like the idea. My Irem isn't overtly magical, but like a perfect version of a city from antiquity. Men were greater then, could do more incredible things straight out of the epic poems. Irem was the city that all since have tried and failed to imitate, the empire that hasn't known a peer before or since. <br /> <br /> The destructive energies of the Rite of Return physically pulled Irem into Duat, and partially erased it from the timestream, <i>a la</i> Atlantis in M:tAs 1e. Relics, the Arisen, and a few ruins still exist, but no amount of digging will ever find Irem itself in North Africa.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":281,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","user":"atamajakki","date":". 01-20-2015, 06:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-20T05:00:02-08:00","id":376066,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/375874-what-s-irem-like-with-your-game-alternate-irems?p=375889#post375889\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you look at it, there's really nothing in common between a Mesoamerican and an Egyptian Mummy beyond the fact that English uses the word &quot;Mummy&quot; to describe each.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I like the idea of Andean mallqui being supernaturals in the WoD, I agree that they shouldn't be the same thing as the Arisen. At least as different as the OWoD's Kindred and Kuei-jin were.<br /> <br /> My own Irem - or the Empire more precisely - covered most of the Middle East, the eastern half of North Africa with some outposts further west, and down south to what's now Ethiopia. The other empire mentioned in the core was in Iran and Central Asia. I picture it having a southern neighbour as well. Most of the empire is gone for good, rendered into Duat - that level of destruction was part of the Rite.<br /> <br /> I also have the Shan'iatu being less powerful than what's depicted in Book of the Deceived. They're special because they accomplished something that ought to have been beyond them, not because they were celestially awesome to start with.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-20T05:18:48-08:00","id":376081,"title":"","content":"I love the idea of other Mummies around the world, spawned as Iremite imitations or original creations. In <b>Mummy: the Curse</b>, though, I like thinking of them <i>all</i> as Lifeless, and only Mummies whose origins are bound up in the Rite of Return are Deathless.<br /> <br /> Still, I think my perspective lines up with the game; after all, when people think of a horror-movie-monster Mummy they think Egyptian, bar none","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-20T17:50:19-08:00","id":376764,"title":"","content":"The only Mummies considered Deathless are the Arisen and Deceived. Tough I don't quite understand what makes one mummy Deathless and the other one Lifeless.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4055-felipe","name":"Felipe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4055_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"538"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-20T18:21:16-08:00","id":376784,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Felipe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/375874-what-s-irem-like-with-your-game-alternate-irems?p=376764#post376764\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The only Mummies considered Deathless are the Arisen and Deceived. Tough I don't quite understand what makes one mummy Deathless and the other one Lifeless.</div> </div> </div> </div>Probably something to so with how every Descent starts a Deathless at Sekhem 10, while Lifeless either have no Sekhem of their own (Amkhata) or have very diminished ratings (Shuankhsen).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-20T18:26:17-08:00","id":376806,"title":"","content":"Memory is also something entirely unique to the Deathless.<br /> <br /> Not all upside...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-20T22:27:38-08:00","id":376944,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Felipe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/375874-what-s-irem-like-with-your-game-alternate-irems?p=376764#post376764\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The only Mummies considered Deathless are the Arisen and Deceived. Tough I don't quite understand what makes one mummy Deathless and the other one Lifeless.</div> </div> </div> </div>Arisen dogma and belief, presumably.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-20T23:11:50-08:00","id":376992,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Christian A</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/375874-what-s-irem-like-with-your-game-alternate-irems?p=376944#post376944\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Arisen dogma and belief, presumably.</div> </div> </div> </div>Or having a working occult apparatus as designed by the Rite of Return's intended use; remember that the Shuankhsen got a less intricate version of the package and the main direct reason we have for their numbers growing is due to fucking up becoming Twice-Arisen.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-20T23:23:47-08:00","id":377001,"title":"","content":"There is a pretty solid case to be made that the forms of Deathless we know of are innately superior to any Lifeless that has thus far appeared in the text.<br /> <br /> The sole apparent advantage the Shuankhsen have over the Deathless are their (comparatively) intact memories, but those come coupled with an automatic derangement and... oh yeah, having your very soul nearly annihilated and the broken fragment that is left enslaved to the embodiment of entropy and destruction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T01:38:09-08:00","id":377046,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Felipe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/375874-what-s-irem-like-with-your-game-alternate-irems?p=376764#post376764\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The only Mummies considered Deathless are the Arisen and Deceived. Tough I don't quite understand what makes one mummy Deathless and the other one Lifeless.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I'm aware of the distinction and exceptional circumstance the Shuankhsen bring in but I didn't want to bog down my post with tediums.<br /> <br /> I think &quot;starting at Sekhem 10&quot; and &quot;has Memory&quot; are viable differences from Lifeless","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T03:35:52-08:00","id":377087,"title":"","content":"I haven't made any customized options for Irem itself yet or the nature of the Empire. But I do know in regards to legacies of the Rite that in my game world, the Mekhet do have an origin tied to attempts at recreation of the Rite, though by that point so much information was lost it was reaction of the Rite attempt than attempt to do SOMETHING.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T04:30:44-08:00","id":377111,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I love the idea of other Mummies around the world, spawned as Iremite imitations or original creations. In Mummy: the Curse, though, I like thinking of them all as Lifeless, and only Mummies whose origins are bound up in the Rite of Return are Deathless. </div> </div> </div>I could also see the Arisen counting various other types of immortal or undead as Lifeless without being part of Mummy: the Curse's official entourage - eternals and harvesters particularly come to mind. Which means that you don't necessarily have to make a mummy from another cultural foundation &quot;lesser&quot; as such; but doing them (and the Deathless) justice probably needs keeping the comparison as far out of your head as you can while putting them together.<br /> <br /> While we're on the subject, while it's not exactly about Irem itself, I do have the relics of Reliquary using Sekhem (Reliquary describes a process happening naturally, which the Shan'iatu in effect learned to industrialise), and various of the Immortals immortals do ultimately derive from Iremite lore (the eternals, wardens, and harvesters). To put it another way, my version of Irem hasn't vanished entirely without trace, even if the traces aren't always recognised for what they are. And there are still ruins to find.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Still, I think my perspective lines up with the game; after all, when people think of a horror-movie-monster Mummy they think Egyptian, bar none. </div> </div> </div>Most people <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":321,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","user":"SunlessNick","date":".  To get line breaks to work 01-22-2015, 09:10 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T04:31:36-08:00","id":377112,"title":"","content":"Trouble with line breaks again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T08:53:54-08:00","id":377258,"title":"","content":"I find it pretty interesting that the <i>Sadikh </i>are considered to be Lifeless; especially considering the heavy &quot;these guys are <b>us</b>&quot; theme these guys have going on. <br /> <br /> (So when they choose (intentionally) to accept an Arisen as their master, where they freely give away their true names, they chain their entire existence to the Arisen - they're only active and alive when their Arisen master is, experiencing something like a virtual reality false life while the Arisen is in Duat. A Sadikh cannot, under any circumstance, disobey his master while in his presence. They get to have powers, yes, but those are only borrowed from the Arisen, who can at any time choose not to let the Sadikh access them. The Sadikh can resurrect, but only if the Arisen allows it.)<br /> <br /> (.. It's a subversion of the whole &quot;Workers, not Kings&quot; theme. The Arisen are to the Sadikh as the Judges are to the Arisen. In a setting in which the Arisen have, essentially, been reduced to eternal service as errand boys for some pretender gods of the underworld, creating a Sadikh is basically their only way of passing on the chains of slavery to others. And yet - as the Arisen get to completely control a human being, heart and soul, by turning him into a Sadikh, they themselves are similarly enslaved by the Judges.). <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T09:02:32-08:00","id":377262,"title":"","content":"Power is the reward for obedience, after all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T12:09:24-08:00","id":377433,"title":"","content":"I, at least, have an idea for Irem's demise. See, the Empire originally lasted till up to the 7th century AD. Then, the rulers of the Nameless Empire did... Something. No source remaining knows exactly what, but whatever it was, it was so wicked and blasphemous that a force so potent that those who recorded these things called it divine not only destroyed the capital with &quot;fire from heaven&quot;, it destroyed the city retroactively, ending Irem shortly after the Rite. All that was left of the first timeline are a few records indicating what happened... And some of them seem to imply this imperfect editing was intentional...<br /> <br /> (I'm trying to keep options open as to what did it. Maybe the Muslims were right, and the Empire was smote by capital-G God. Maybe it was the Machine or Promethean's Principle, acting according to their strange and alien goals. Maybe it was an Archmage who didn't like Irem or who wanted to restore the timeline that came before the last. Who knows?)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T12:43:30-08:00","id":377440,"title":"","content":"Speaking of Sadikh - I was recently confused by them, and was considering starting a new thread to clarify, but I hope you don't mind if I take the chance to ask about it here.<br /> From what I can tell, Sadikh (and the ability to make them) aren't even mentioned until the Storytelling section. Are they meant to be an option for player characters? and should there be any costs for them beyond the dot of Willpower? I would think you would need a retainer-like merit and possibly spend some exp on the ritual for them, but nothing along these lines is mentioned.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T14:51:31-08:00","id":377553,"title":"","content":"For Sadikh, it clearly depends on the structure of the game. If you want to run a game with one Mummy, a ghost/revenant, and a Sadikh, you're obviously going to be letting players look at particular segments of the Storytelling section. They're only going to be an option for player character if that is the format the storyteller chooses. It is one supported by the game rules, but not the default.<br /> <br /> Personally, I had a player in my game who wanted to take a Retainer anyway. I asked him if he wanted to pay the extra dot to make it a Witness Retainer, and when he said yes, offered a Sadikh as an alternative (plus the extra 8 experience for the Willpower dot). So in order to purchase one, it cost the Retainer merit, plus an extra dot for the Witness upgrade, plus eight experience to represent the Willpower dot. He reckons it was a fantastic deal. Sadikh are explicitly listed as Witnesses, so I just assume you build them like a Witness Retainer and take away the owning character's Willpower. Seems like a reasonable cost for me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ZealousChristian24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/375874-what-s-irem-like-with-your-game-alternate-irems?p=377433#post377433\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I, at least, have an idea for Irem's demise. See, the Empire originally lasted till up to the 7th century AD. Then, the rulers of the Nameless Empire did... Something. No source remaining knows exactly what, but whatever it was, it was so wicked and blasphemous that a force so potent that those who recorded these things called it divine not only destroyed the capital with &quot;fire from heaven&quot;, it destroyed the city retroactively, ending Irem shortly after the Rite. All that was left of the first timeline are a few records indicating what happened... And some of them seem to imply this imperfect editing was intentional...<br /> <br /> (I'm trying to keep options open as to what did it. Maybe the Muslims were right, and the Empire was smote by capital-G God. Maybe it was the Machine or Promethean's Principle, acting according to their strange and alien goals. Maybe it was an Archmage who didn't like Irem or who wanted to restore the timeline that came before the last. Who knows?)</div> </div> </div> </div>That's an interesting option. I personally prefer the link between Irem's stated presence in the timeline and the aridification of the Sahara. Any particular reason why you wanted it to last an extra 4,700 years?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T18:07:27-08:00","id":377647,"title":"","content":"I like a relatively diverse Irem, at least in its early and middle periods. I decided Ubar was the sumerian werewolf and scelesti hotbed, and when it fell to Irem, it added a dash of protosumerian/babylonian flavor to Irem in general but especially Eastern Irem. There was an ongoing tug of war in the South, with Nubia/Kush and its invasion being central to a lot of flashbacks. Like Atamajakki, my Irem also had conflicts with the horse nomads in kazakhstan. Irem, like most other empires, failed when it came to fully dominating Afghanistan. They did engage in peaceful trade with proto India and proto China. Iremite colonies existed in the mediterranean islands, though the &quot;sea people&quot; kept them from conquering Greece or Italty. Irem had lots of flavor variation between city-states, though aspects of egypt and babylon are found throughout. Iremite explorers had reached northern europe and asia, but never got beyond outposts. Im still unsure just how much undeath was rampant, but i suspect nenitu slaves, ancestor cults, and the occassional duat-thing should come into the picture. So far, though, the flashbacks have been fairly simplistic in terms of culture setting, as its more focused on the romance between a mummy and his now-reincarnate slave lover. Im looking forward to Sothis Ascends, cuz iirc there will be more solid details on irem and its successors in Uruk and Egypt.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T19:47:51-08:00","id":377731,"title":"","content":"@Azahul: Mostly the date was because, if I remember correctly, Islam came into being around the 6th century, and the first mention of a &quot;Iram, who had lofty pillars&quot; was in a book of the Koran(see &quot;the Muslims were right&quot; in the list of theories I suggested).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T19:59:48-08:00","id":377743,"title":"","content":"I know of the mention, although I will confess to not being fully aware of the surrounding context. I was under the vague impression that it's referred to in the Qur'an as a city was destroyed at an unspecified point in the past, meaning that you don't necessarily need to have had it last up until the advent of Islam. Hence the interest in whether there was any additional reasoning. Or my understanding of the quotation could be entirely wrong.<br /> <br /> Anyway, either way is interesting. Certainly novel. People like to play around and change the information on Irem in the core book a lot, but you're the first person I've seen who decided to have it last so far past the Rite of Return.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1059,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","user":"Azahul","date":". 01-21-2015, 11:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T22:25:06-08:00","id":377810,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cleverest of Things</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/375874-what-s-irem-like-with-your-game-alternate-irems?p=377647#post377647\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like a relatively diverse Irem, at least in its early and middle periods. I decided Ubar was the sumerian werewolf and scelesti hotbed, and when it fell to Irem, it added a dash of protosumerian/babylonian flavor to Irem in general but especially Eastern Irem. There was an ongoing tug of war in the South, with Nubia/Kush and its invasion being central to a lot of flashbacks. Like Atamajakki, my Irem also had conflicts with the horse nomads in kazakhstan. Irem, like most other empires, failed when it came to fully dominating Afghanistan. They did engage in peaceful trade with proto India and proto China. Iremite colonies existed in the mediterranean islands, though the &quot;sea people&quot; kept them from conquering Greece or Italty. Irem had lots of flavor variation between city-states, though aspects of egypt and babylon are found throughout. Iremite explorers had reached northern europe and asia, but never got beyond outposts. Im still unsure just how much undeath was rampant, but i suspect nenitu slaves, ancestor cults, and the occassional duat-thing should come into the picture. So far, though, the flashbacks have been fairly simplistic in terms of culture setting, as its more focused on the romance between a mummy and his now-reincarnate slave lover. Im looking forward to Sothis Ascends, cuz iirc there will be more solid details on irem and its successors in Uruk and Egypt.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm glad I'm not the only one that has Irem fighting with Ubar, potentially drawing from the Sumerian werewolf god-kings in the <i>Forsaken Chronicler's Guide. </i>And I definitely have an appropriately mythic proto-China (perhaps the Xia Dynasty?) that they had distant trade with. <br /> <br /> How far do you guys Iremite explorers making it to? I'm torn on whether or not they made it to the Americas or not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T22:26:37-08:00","id":377811,"title":"","content":"Northern Turkey? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Honestly, anything outside of the City of Pillars itself is barely relevant for my game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-22T05:31:09-08:00","id":377972,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I don't think my Nameless Empire ever settled anything north of the Mediterranean. The Arabian peninsula's harsh clime did their explorers no favors, but they certainly had outposts along the Fertile Crescent. As for the rest of Africa, Morocco might be too far but it's a possibility, and they certainly settled as far south as Ethiopia.<br /> <br /> My girlfriend's family is from Syria and, seeing as she's my only <b>Mummy</b> player, let it be no surprise that the world-shaking relic she finds is housed in an Iremite fortress there <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-22T06:30:28-08:00","id":377996,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/375874-what-s-irem-like-with-your-game-alternate-irems?p=377810#post377810\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm glad I'm not the only one that has Irem fighting with Ubar, potentially drawing from the Sumerian werewolf god-kings in the <i>Forsaken Chronicler's Guide. </i>And I definitely have an appropriately mythic proto-China (perhaps the Xia Dynasty?) that they had distant trade with.</div> </div> </div> </div>While modern &quot;this is the truth behind the myths&quot; theories tend to conflate Irem and Ubar, that's not really consistent with the source material (for one thing, Ubar was a legendary region, not a legendary city). So making them different polities works better, I agree. As I said earlier on, I personally put Ubar in Central Asia rather than Mesopotamia or Arabia - the reason the names show up particularly in Arabian legendry is that that's where they <i>met</i>, and thus where the natives had most reason to refer to them distinctively by name.<br /> <br /> Speaking of which, one thing that is different about my own Irem from the canon: Irem was the city's, and by extension the empires, actual name; it's not a placeholder. The sacrifice of its name is more along the lines of how the Arisen's names are held in Duat - they're not gone and unknowable, they just belong to someone other than those to whom they refer - so it is with Irem.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How far do you guys Iremite explorers making it to? I'm torn on whether or not they made it to the Americas or not. </div> </div> </div>Mm, if they were stalled to the east by Ubar, which is canon, I think it's a bit odd to have them stretch limitlessly in every other direction. Keeping it roughly on a par with the greatest extent of how far Egypt reached makes for a better tone IMO. Into a lot of North and East Africa, probably, even if they didn't colonise there; same with the coast of Europe - the Middle East, some of Turkey - but not India, Asia, the Americas, or the Pacific.<br /> <br /> I mentioned earlier that I have the Shan'iatu being a lot less powerful than they seem in the Book of the Deceived. A reason I didn't mention, but which is part of my thinking, is that I like that part of Irem's success was down to its technological advantage, so I don't want the magic to be so powerful as to render that meaningless. It's an interesting comparison: Irem was a place where new technologies abounded, where a new medium for communication was revolutionising society, and where concepts that hadn't existed a century before were a part of daily life; no wonder mummies can adjust to the present.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-22T09:26:12-08:00","id":378132,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/375874-what-s-irem-like-with-your-game-alternate-irems?p=377810#post377810\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How far do you guys Iremite explorers making it to? I'm torn on whether or not they made it to the Americas or not.</div> </div> </div> </div> I like to think that they may have(if Egyptians could, then Irem probably had the tech), but never really seriously. They had too much stuff on their plate with more local issues, so they probably just grabbed luxuries like Tobacco and headed off to deal with more important things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-24T19:01:19-08:00","id":380664,"title":"","content":"Ubar was revealed to be sumeria region by the name of its people, Kir En Gar. The only results I could find for that seemed to be proto babylonian. However, the extent of it couldve been large, and it couldve had outposts pretty much anywhere Irem did. As for the Americas, I feel the existence of certain relics (like the star-scroll thing) allowed potential explorers to go beyond mortal reach, but doubtfully enough to make anything more than an outpost akin to the ones in antarctica. In my game, they DID entomb a mummy or two there, but the builders of the tomb were converted natives and the priests who did the ritual were entomed with him as Uter. Of course, I had this plan before Deceived was out. With the way they can relocate, that has given me new options to explore with American mummies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}